//
// A call does not take type arguments: `first<integer>(a)` is refused, saying that the call
// infers them and naming the cure where an argument does not fix one (@PLN165 `D-Infer`).
//
// Type arguments are written in TYPE position only (`p: Pair<integer, text>`, where `<`
// already means what it means in `vector<integer>`); in an expression `<` is a comparison, so
// the call read as the chain `first < integer > (a)` and was refused as "comparison operators
// do not chain".  A local variable keeps the comparison reading.
// @falsified-at: 04f5791cf — exit 1 -> 0, expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1
//   CHANNEL: expectations (the refusal is what moves; the program is refused on both trees).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a call does not take type arguments — `first(…)` infers them from its arguments; where an argument does not fix one, give the value a declared type (`a: vector<integer> = []`)

fn first<T>(v: vector<T>) -> T { v[0] }

fn main() {
  a: vector<integer> = [1, 2];
  println("{first<integer>(a)}");
}
